<section class="cs-section">
  <h2 class="cs-section-title">Overview</h2>
  <div class="cs-section-body">
    <p>The performance module allows organisations to conduct reviews of their employees' performance. The system is built around three key entities: <strong>Templates, Cycles,</strong> and <strong>Review phases</strong>.</p>
    <p>Three personas interact with the system — Admins who configure reviews, Reviewees (employees being reviewed), and Reviewers (managers, peers, subordinates). The core design tension: <strong>power for admins vs. clarity for end users</strong>.</p>
    <p>What makes this uniquely difficult is the visibility matrix — who sees what, and when. A reviewee or reviewer seeing the wrong thing at the wrong moment breaks trust in the entire system.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cs-section">
  <h2 class="cs-section-title">Business Objectives</h2>
  <div class="cs-section-body">
    <ul class="cs-list">
      <li><strong>Acquisition:</strong> Incentivise organisations to switch to OmniHR by offering an integrated performance module.</li>
      <li><strong>Retention:</strong> Encourage stickiness — no need to get a separate appraisal software.</li>
      <li><strong>Design outcome:</strong> Good enough features to meet the majority of needs. Not aiming to be the best appraisal software — it's not the key differentiation factor.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cs-section">
  <h2 class="cs-section-title">Process</h2>
  <div class="cs-section-body">
    <ol class="cs-list cs-list--ordered">
      <li>Competitive research across appraisal softwares — noting what we liked, what was missing, and what features we could match or surpass.</li>
      <li>Defined scope for each iteration with stakeholders.</li>
      <li>Mapped user journeys for the end-to-end performance cycle: templates and cycles, launching reviews, and viewing results. Key wireframes for initial ideas.</li>
      <li>Once aligned on strategy, moved to hi-fi designs and dev hand-off presentations.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cs-section">
  <h2 class="cs-section-title">Deep Dive: Visibility Rules</h2>
  <div class="cs-section-body">
    <p>When expanding from Self &amp; Manager Review to include Peer &amp; Upward Review, we introduced visibility rules. The key questions: whose responses can the reviewee and manager see, and when? Are peer and subordinate identities anonymous or visible?</p>
    <p>The challenge was covering all scenarios for two main user roles. We solved it by mapping every combination, and sped up the process by reusing components.</p>

    <h3 class="cs-subsection-title">How it works</h3>
    <p>A new step in the review cycle creation: <strong>Step visibility rules</strong></p>
    <ol class="cs-list">
      <li><strong>"Can they view results"</strong> lets admins choose if the reviewee and manager can see each persona's responses, and if so, when.</li>
      <li><strong>"Can they view the name of the reviewer"</strong> controls whether respondent identities are visible.</li>
    </ol>

    <figure class="cs-figure">
      <img src="/assets/perf-module-1.png" alt="Set visibility rules step">
      <figcaption class="cs-caption">Set visibility rules step</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>For certain personas, the first option's selection constrains the second — creating dependent logic.</p>

    <figure class="cs-figure">
      <img src="/assets/perf-module-2.png" alt="Options on the left side affect the right side">
      <figcaption class="cs-caption">Options on the left side affect the right side</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>We mapped all visibility rule combinations and used them to align on interactions and views across user roles, and sped up the process by reusing components.</p>
    <p>Two examples of the visibility rules mapping. We used them also to agree on the interactions and views for the various user roles.</p>

    <figure class="cs-figure">
      <img src="/assets/perf-module-3.png" alt="Mapping of all visibility scenarios">
      <figcaption class="cs-caption">Mapping of all visibility scenarios</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>Example of all possible variants of the right-side component, which is nested within the card component.</p>
    <p>It covers all the scenarios for the various review types, eg. Self review, peer selection, etc..</p>

    <figure class="cs-figure">
      <img src="/assets/perf-module-4.png" alt="Variants of the right-side nested component">
      <figcaption class="cs-caption">Variants of the right-side nested component</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cs-section">
  <h2 class="cs-section-title">Deep Dive: Peer & Upward Review</h2>
  <div class="cs-section-body">
    <p>The introduction of peer and upward review meant previous designs couldn't simply be edited. We needed to redesign the to-do page, review cycle listing, and details page. We overcame the lack of exploration time by referencing how competitor softwares handled it.</p>

    <h3 class="cs-subsection-title">Before</h3>
  
    <p>At the performance cycle listing page, there was only self and manager review to consider for each row item.</p>

    <figure class="cs-figure">
      <img src="/assets/perf-module-5.png" alt="Performance cycle listing page with only self and manager review">
      <figcaption class="cs-caption">Performance cycle listing page (BEFORE)</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>At the performance details page, review summary cards are not fully utilising the available space; columns showing status for each participant’s self and manager review.</p>

    <figure class="cs-figure">
      <img src="/assets/perf-module-6.png" alt="Performance cycle listing page with only self and manager review">
      <figcaption class="cs-caption">Performance cycle details page (BEFORE)</figcaption>
    </figure>
    
    <p>We initially built a dedicated page for the performance reviews tasks which was not consistent with other tasks, and caused some confusion for the users. Also, there was no more space to fit in the new reviewer types with this design.</p>
    
    <figure class="cs-figure">
      <img src="/assets/perf-module-7.png" alt="My Reviews page for reviewing managers">
      <figcaption class="cs-caption">My Reviews page for reviewing managers (BEFORE)</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h3 class="cs-subsection-title">After</h3>

    <p>In order to display statuses for up to 5 review types + 2 selection periods, we'd decided to switched from a inline view to a stacked layout — Row 1 for essential cycle details, Row 2 for review statuses.</p>
    
    <figure class="cs-figure">
      <img src="/assets/perf-module-8.png" alt="My Reviews page for reviewing managers">
      <figcaption class="cs-caption">Performance cycle listing page (AFTER)</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>One of the most important change we did was to make the review status cards similar between the listing and details page, such as the position and style of the review type title, period and status badge.</p>

    <p>For better clarity of progress, we moved the manager to its own column, and used icons for the statuses instead of badges.</p>
    <p>The counter allowed the admin and HR manager to see progress of review types with multiple parties.</p>
   
    <figure class="cs-figure">
      <img src="/assets/perf-module-9.png" alt="My Reviews page for reviewing managers">
      <figcaption class="cs-caption">Performance cycle details page (AFTER)</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>New task page tabs: <strong>"My Review"</strong> for reviews and responses related to me, and <strong>"Team Review"</strong> for everything outside of me — subordinates, peers, direct manager.</p>

    <p>By stacking the review type cards, it made skimming lot easier. For each review type, the user is able to know the person(s) involved, and the statuses and CTAs.</p>
    <p> For the CTAs, we used primary button for strong ones like Take Review, Outline button for weak ones like viewing the responses.</p>

    <figure class="cs-figure">
      <img src="/assets/perf-module-10.png" alt="My Reviews page for reviewing managers">
      <figcaption class="cs-caption">New performance review task page with tabs for Me Review and Team Review (AFTER)</figcaption>
    </figure>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="cs-section">
  <h2 class="cs-section-title">Reflection</h2>
  <div class="cs-section-body">
    <ul class="cs-list">
      <li><strong>Would do differently:</strong> Use a different layout for the My Review and Team Review cards to reduce wasted space.</li>
      <li><strong>Current limitation:</strong> The system still can't cleanly handle employees who miss submission deadlines or extend deadlines for specific individuals — this requires backend manipulation by developers.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>
